<idea-plugin>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Identity                                                           -->
    <!-- The id, name and vendor are declared here and nowhere else. The    -->
    <!-- version and compatibility range are worked out in build.gradle.kts -->
    <!-- (intellijPlatform.pluginConfiguration) and patched in at build.    -->
    <!-- The change notes are patched in too, from CHANGELOG.md.            -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <id>org.testin</id>
    <name>Testin</name>
    <vendor email="mtb550@gmail.com" url="https://github.com/MutebAlMughyiri/test-in">Muteb AlMughyiri</vendor>
    <resource-bundle>messages</resource-bundle>
    <description><![CDATA[
    <b>Testin</b> brings test case management into the IDE. Design test cases, run them, and report on the results in the same window as the code they cover.
    <br/><br/>
    Everything is stored as plain files in a folder you choose &mdash; readable, diffable, and versioned with Git if you want them to be. There is no server to run, no account to create, and no data leaves the machine.
    <br/><br/>

    <b>Design</b>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Test project tree.</b> Test projects, test sets, packages and test runs in a sidebar tree, with create, rename, copy, cut, paste, drag and drop, and undo &mdash; the operations a file tree already taught your hands.</li>
        <li><b>Test case editor.</b> Description, steps, expected result, priority, groups, module, preconditions, reference and test data. Search, filter and paging keep a large suite navigable, and every attribute can be switched off the view when it is not the one you are working on.</li>
        <li><b>Spreadsheet grid.</b> The same test cases as a table: multi-cell selection, copy and paste with Excel in both directions, rows that size themselves to their content, and column widths that survive a refresh.</li>
        <li><b>Bulk editing.</b> Descriptions, steps, expected results, groups and more, changed across a whole selection in one dialog.</li>
        <li><b>Details panel.</b> The full test case beside the list, including who created it and when.</li>
    </ul>

    <b>Execute</b>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Test runs.</b> Choose test cases into a cycle and record what happened: Passed, Failed or Blocked, with an actual result, bug severity and bug priority on a failure, and per-test-case durations timed for you. Test cases a test run never reached are marked Untested when it closes, and a test case deleted after it was executed keeps the result it earned, marked Removed.</li>
        <li><b>Automation</b> <i>(with the Java and TestNG plugins)</i><b>.</b> Generate packages, classes and methods from your test cases, run them by class or by method, and move between a test case and its code from a gutter icon or a click on the card.</li>
    </ul>

    <b>Share</b>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Reports.</b> Execution reports as HTML, PDF, Word or Excel, with the pass rate, a table per status, and the test run's real execution times.</li>
        <li><b>Import and export.</b> Excel, CSV and JSON in and out, plus HTML on the way out. Mapped column by column, with a preview before anything is written.</li>
        <li><b>Git</b> <i>(with the Git plugin)</i><b>.</b> A review dialog listing every pending change &mdash; test cases field by field, test runs, and the markers that make a folder a test set &mdash; then commit and push from the same place. Clone a shared test project, sync with a rebase, revert a single changed field, and switch branches from the panel.</li>
    </ul>

    <b>Compatibility</b>
    <ul>
        <li>IntelliJ IDEA, PyCharm, Rider, GoLand, WebStorm and other IntelliJ-based IDEs, build 262 and later.</li>
        <li>Test management works in all of them. Java code generation, TestNG execution and the Git features appear when those plugins are installed, and are absent rather than broken when they are not.</li>
    </ul>

    <b>Privacy</b>
    <ul>
        <li>No telemetry, no accounts, no analytics. Testin talks to the Git remote you configure yourself; when you press <b>Report Bug</b>, to the GitHub repository your own <b>testin.yml</b> names, through the <b>gh</b> sign-in already on your machine; and, when you have connected an AI agent and press <b>Automate Test Case</b>, to that agent's own command line on your machine, carrying one test case and nothing else. Testin keeps no API key - an agent reads its own from your system environment. Nothing reaches the developer.</li>
    </ul>

    <b>Team</b>
    <ul>
        <li><b><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/mtb550/">Muteb AlMughyiri</a></b> &mdash; author and maintainer</li>
        <li><b><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/maalzamil/">Mohammed AlZamil</a></b> &mdash; testing and feature requests</li>
    </ul>

    <b>Support</b>
    <ul>
        <li>How Testin works: <a href="https://mutebalmughyiri.github.io/test-in/">mutebalmughyiri.github.io/test-in</a> &mdash; what it does, how each screen works and the rules it keeps</li>
        <li>Questions and ideas: <a href="https://github.com/MutebAlMughyiri/test-in/discussions">github.com/MutebAlMughyiri/test-in/discussions</a></li>
        <li>Issues and feature requests: <a href="https://github.com/MutebAlMughyiri/test-in/issues">github.com/MutebAlMughyiri/test-in/issues</a></li>
    </ul>
]]></description>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Dependencies                                                       -->
    <!-- Only the platform is required. Java, TestNG and Git are optional   -->
    <!-- so Testin loads in PyCharm/GoLand/WebStorm; the code checks        -->
    <!-- org.testin.services.OptionalPlugin before touching their classes.  -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <depends>com.intellij.modules.platform</depends>
    <!-- Every IDE has the language support and JetBrains Gateway, remote    -->
    <!-- development's launcher, does not, so this leaves the launcher out   -->
    <!-- (#419). Naming Gateway's id would leave out IntelliJ IDEA too: it   -->
    <!-- bundles the Remote Development Gateway plugin under the same id.    -->
    <depends>com.intellij.modules.lang</depends>
    <depends optional="true" config-file="testin-java.xml">com.intellij.modules.java</depends>
    <depends optional="true" config-file="testin-testng.xml">TestNG-J</depends>
    <depends optional="true" config-file="testin-git.xml">Git4Idea</depends>
    <depends optional="true" config-file="testin-json.xml">com.intellij.modules.json</depends>
    <depends optional="true" config-file="testin-terminal.xml">org.jetbrains.plugins.terminal</depends>

    <!-- JetBrains Client, remote development's thin front end and Code     -->
    <!-- With Me's guest, holds no project and ships none of the Git4Idea   -->
    <!-- classes the Git code calls (#419). Testin runs in the host IDE.    -->
    <!-- The IDE the inspection reads holds no such module, so it cannot    -->
    <!-- resolve the id; the Plugin Verifier and the Marketplace do.        -->
    <!--suppress PluginXmlValidity -->
    <incompatible-with>com.intellij.jetbrains.client</incompatible-with>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Content modules                                                    -->
    <!-- Code that needs an optional plugin lives in a module of its own,   -->
    <!-- which declares that dependency itself. The Plugin Verifier then    -->
    <!-- checks the module only against IDEs that have it, instead of       -->
    <!-- reporting 53 unresolved classes against every IDE without Java     -->
    <!-- - which is what the Marketplace shows on the plugin page (#144).   -->
    <!-- loading="optional": an IDE without the plugin skips the module.    -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Rule-CODEGEN-061 -->
    <content>
        <module name="Testin.testin-java" loading="optional"/>
        <module name="Testin.testin-testng" loading="optional"/>
        <module name="Testin.testin-apimodel" loading="optional"/>
        <module name="Testin.testin-robot" loading="optional"/>
    </content>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- What a content module contributes back                             -->
    <!-- The core cannot call into a module, so a module hands its work     -->
    <!-- over here. An IDE without the module leaves the list empty, which  -->
    <!-- every reader treats as an answer rather than an absence (#144).    -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <extensionPoints>
        <extensionPoint name="testRunners"
                        interface="org.testin.runner.AutomationExecutor"
                        dynamic="true"/>
        <extensionPoint name="codeGenerators"
                        interface="org.testin.codegen.CodeChangeRegistry"
                        dynamic="true"/>
        <extensionPoint name="codeNavigation"
                        interface="org.testin.navigate.TestMethodCode"
                        dynamic="true"/>
        <extensionPoint name="testMethodFiles"
                        interface="org.testin.codegen.agent.TestMethodFile"
                        dynamic="true"/>
        <extensionPoint name="apiModelMakers"
                        interface="org.testin.codegen.ApiModelMaker"
                        dynamic="true"/>
    </extensionPoints>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Extensions (platform-only; Java-dependent ones live in             -->
    <!-- testin-java.xml)                                                   -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.intellij">
        <!-- Notices test data changing on disk - a hand edit, a pull, a branch
             switch - and reads the projects that changed again (#20) -->
        <vfs.asyncListener implementation="org.testin.indexer.TestinFileWatcher"/>

        <!-- The Testin Help mark in the status bar (UC-INTERNAL-009) -->
        <statusBarWidgetFactory id="testin.help" implementation="org.testin.help.HelpMarkFactory" order="last"/>

        <!-- First run activity -->
        <postStartupActivity
                implementation="org.testin.explorer.TestinStartup"
        />


        <fileEditorProvider
                implementation="org.testin.editor.open.UnifiedEditorProvider"
        />

        <!-- Editor Drop Handler -->
        <fileDropHandler
                implementation="org.testin.explorer.tree.TreeDropHandler"
        />

        <!-- Services -->
        <applicationService
                serviceImplementation="org.testin.setting.AppSettingsState"
        />

        <!-- Tool windows -->
        <!-- Rule-TREE-PANEL-014 -->
        <toolWindow
                id="testin.tree"
                factoryClass="org.testin.explorer.TreePanelToolWindowFactory"
                anchor="left"
                icon="org.testin.util.Icons.PLUGIN"
                secondary="false"
                doNotActivateOnStart="true"
        />

        <!-- Rule-VIEW-PANEL-001 -->
        <toolWindow
                id="testin.view"
                factoryClass="org.testin.view.ViewToolWindowFactory"
                anchor="right"
                icon="org.testin.util.Icons.PLUGIN"
                secondary="false"
                doNotActivateOnStart="true"
        />

        <!-- Notifications -->
        <notificationGroup
                id="testin.notifications"
                displayType="BALLOON"
                isLogByDefault="true"
                bundle="messages"
                key="testin.display.name"
        />

        <!-- Settings: application-level, because the state behind the page always
             was. One page for the IDE, so two open projects cannot hold two sets
             of fields over one settings file and overwrite each other (#70). -->
        <applicationConfigurable
                instance="org.testin.setting.SettingsConfigurable"
                id="org.testin.setting.SettingsConfigurable"
                key="testin.display.name"
                bundle="messages"
                groupId="tools"
        />

        <applicationConfigurable
                instance="org.testin.setting.AgentSettingsConfigurable"
                id="org.testin.setting.AgentSettingsConfigurable"
                parentId="org.testin.setting.SettingsConfigurable"
                key="agent.page.name"
                bundle="messages"
        />

        <applicationConfigurable
                instance="org.testin.setting.AgentPromptConfigurable"
                id="org.testin.setting.AgentPromptConfigurable"
                parentId="org.testin.setting.AgentSettingsConfigurable"
                key="agent.prompt.page.name"
                bundle="messages"
        />

    </extensions>

    <!-- Every action a tester can reach, declared so Find Action offers it by
         name and Settings -> Keymap can rebind its key (#119).

         An action with a <keyboard-shortcut> owns that key across the IDE and
         grays itself outside Testin. An action without one is still declared:
         it is listed in the Keymap with no default. The surface it belongs to
         registers the key on it, because a declared shortcut is dispatched
         before a component's input map. Declared, it would take ENTER, DELETE or
         the three clipboard keys away from the tree, the card list or the grid.
         Rule-INTERNAL-066, and docs/shortcuts.md says which keys are which.

         The search was the first of these and is still the plainest: it has to
         open from the tree, from either editor and from the view panel alike,
         so it lives in the keymap (#29).

         A key the IDE already owns is a key Testin loses. The platform gathers
         every action bound to a keystroke, runs whichever one is enabled, and
         says nothing when two are - and a component input map cannot rescue it,
         because a registered shortcut is dispatched first. That is how
         CTRL+SHIFT+C quietly did CopyPaths in a test set editor for weeks.
         DeclaredShortcutsTest reads this file to check no two Testin actions
         ever claim one key by accident.

         Copy, Cut and Paste Test Case carry no key at all. They were moved off
         CTRL+SHIFT to ALT+SHIFT to stop losing that fight, and then the fight
         turned out not to be worth having: they are context menu entries, and
         a tester who wants them clicks them. They are still declared, so Find
         Action offers them and a tester who does want a key can set one. -->
    <!-- Rule-INTERNAL-071, Rule-PRODUCT-015, Rule-PRODUCT-017, Rule-PRODUCT-018 -->
    <actions>
        <action
                id="Testin.Search"
                class="org.testin.search.GlobalSearchAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Search"
        >
            <!-- Ctrl+Alt+F is IntroduceField in the IDE's own keymap, Cmd+Alt+F on
                 a Mac, and it is kept anyway: in a Java file the refactoring
                 answers, in the Testin panel the search does, and a tester who
                 does not refactor Java never meets the other one. Ctrl+Alt+Shift+F
                 is free if it ever bites. docs/shortcuts.md says the same, and
                 Rule-INTERNAL-069 asks for this note. -->
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="ctrl alt F"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="Mac OS X 10.5+" first-keystroke="meta alt F"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="Mac OS X" first-keystroke="meta alt F"/>
        </action>

        <!-- The tree's actions. Declared rather than constructed so Find Action
             finds them and Settings -> Keymap can rebind them; each reads what
             the tree has selected from TestinDataKeys and grays itself everywhere
             else, so a key declared here is inert outside Testin (#119). -->
        <!-- The editors' actions. Each reads what the editor has selected from
             TestinDataKeys and grays itself outside one (#119). -->
        <!-- UC-CODEGEN-022: New -> Testin API Model from JSON, below Java Class.
             testin-apimodel answers it through apiModelMakers; with no Java
             plugin it is gray and says why, as every code action is. -->
        <action
                id="Testin.ApiModel"
                class="org.testin.codegen.ApiModelAction"
                icon="AllIcons.FileTypes.Json">
            <add-to-group group-id="NewGroup" anchor="after" relative-to-action="NewClass"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.CreateTestCase"
                class="org.testin.testcase.CreateTestCaseAction"
                icon="org.testin.util.Icons.TEST_CASE_LETTER"
        >
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="ctrl M"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="Mac OS X 10.5+" first-keystroke="ctrl alt meta M"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="Mac OS X" first-keystroke="ctrl alt meta M"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.UpdateTestCase"
                class="org.testin.testcase.UpdateTestCaseAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Edit"
        >
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="F2"/>
        </action>

        <!-- One entry per run item status, generated from RunItemStatus. No key
             here: P, F and B carry no modifier, so a keymap entry would answer
             them everywhere in the IDE - the test run editor's list binds them
             (#119). -->
        <group
                id="Testin.SetRunItemStatus"
                class="org.testin.testrun.SetRunItemStatusGroup"
                popup="false"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.UpdateRunItem"
                class="org.testin.testrun.EditFailureDetailAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Edit"
        >
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="F2"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.RemoveTestCase"
                class="org.testin.testcase.RemoveTestCaseAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.DeleteTag"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.PasteTestCaseNode"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.PasteTestCaseAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.MenuPaste"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.CopyTestCaseNode"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.CopyTestCaseAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Copy"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.CutTestCaseNode"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.CutTestCaseAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.MenuCut"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.CopyTestCase"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.CopyTestCaseValueAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Copy"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.AutomateTestCase"
                class="org.testin.codegen.AutomateTestCaseAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.IntentionBulb"
        >
            <!-- F12 is JumpToLastWindow in the IDE's own keymap, and it is kept
                 anyway: a shared key is fine where it works, and no code goes in
                 to win it. CTRL+F12 was worse - FileStructurePopup owns that, and
                 on a Mac it owns CMD+F12 too, so the Mac key is CTRL+ALT+CMD+F12,
                 which nothing else uses. It replaces the F12 the Mac keymaps would
                 otherwise inherit. Rule-INTERNAL-069 asks for this note. -->
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="F12"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="Mac OS X 10.5+" first-keystroke="ctrl alt meta F12" replace-all="true"/>
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="Mac OS X" first-keystroke="ctrl alt meta F12" replace-all="true"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.RunTestMethod"
                class="org.testin.quickaction.ExecuteOrStopTestMethodsAction"
                icon="AllIcons.RunConfigurations.TestState.Run"
        >
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="F5"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.NavigateToTestMethod"
                class="org.testin.quickaction.NavigateToTestMethodAction"
                icon="AllIcons.General.ArrowRight"
        >
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="shift F5"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.NavigateToTestCase"
                class="org.testin.quickaction.NavigateToTestCaseAction"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.ViewDetails"
                class="org.testin.view.ViewDetailsAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.PreviewDetails"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.CreateNode"
                class="org.testin.creator.CreateTreeNodeAction"
                icon="AllIcons.General.Add"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.EditTestRun"
                class="org.testin.testrun.EditTestRunAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Edit"
        >
            <!-- The third action on F2, and the same argument as the two before
                 it: F2 changes the thing in front of you, and which thing that
                 is depends on where you are standing. Update Test Case answers
                 in the test set editor, Failed Test Case Details in the test
                 run editor, and this one in the tree. Each is gray where the others answer,
                 so only one can ever take the key. -->
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="F2"/>
        </action>

        <action
                id="Testin.ReCreateTestRun"
                class="org.testin.testrun.ReCreateTestRunAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Refresh"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.Open"
                class="org.testin.open.OpenAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.MenuOpen"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.ShowNodeDetails"
                class="org.testin.view.marker.ShowNodeDetailsAction"
                icon="AllIcons.General.IndentDetected"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.Export"
                class="org.testin.importexport.exports.ExportAction"
                icon="AllIcons.ToolbarDecorator.Export"
        />

        <!-- Declared here rather than in testin-git.xml, so the action exists in
             every IDE and grays itself with the reason when Git is missing -
             the same answer the tree menu gives (#273). -->
        <action
                id="Testin.ViewPendingCommits"
                class="org.testin.git.review.ViewPendingCommitsAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Commit"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.SyncWithRemote"
                class="org.testin.git.SyncWithRemoteAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.SyncPanels"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.Import"
                class="org.testin.importexport.imports.ImportAction"
                icon="AllIcons.ToolbarDecorator.Import"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.RunTests"
                class="org.testin.runner.ExecuteTestsAction"
                icon="AllIcons.RunConfigurations.TestState.Run"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.OrderNode"
                class="org.testin.order.OrderNodeAction"
                icon="AllIcons.ObjectBrowser.Sorted"
        />

        <!-- One entry per status the selected node has, generated from its
             enum rather than written out here: a status is a constant on
             TestProjectStatus, TestSetStatus or PackageStatus, and a second list of
             them in this file is one that goes stale the day a status is added
             (#175, C9). One group rather than one per kind of node - the node
             says which statuses it has (#110). Compact, so the entries sit
             directly in the Actions menu the way they always have. -->
        <group
                id="Testin.UpdateNodeStatus"
                class="org.testin.explorer.tree.UpdateNodeStatusGroup"
                popup="false"
        />

        <!-- No keyboard-shortcut: DELETE is the tree's key, the card list's and
             the grid's, and one keymap entry would answer for all three. The
             tree binds it on this action instead (#119). -->
        <action
                id="Testin.RemoveNode"
                class="org.testin.remove.RemoveAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.GC"
        />

        <!-- No keyboard-shortcut on either: CTRL+C and CTRL+X are the grid's
             keys for its own cells and the card list's for a test case, and a
             registered shortcut is dispatched before a component's input map -
             so a keymap entry here would answer for all three. The tree binds
             them on these very actions instead (#119). -->
        <action
                id="Testin.CopyNode"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.CopyNodeAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Copy"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.CutNode"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.CutNodeAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.MenuCut"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.PasteNode"
                class="org.testin.clipboard.PasteNodeAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.MenuPaste"
        />

        <action
                id="Testin.Rename"
                class="org.testin.rename.RenameAction"
                icon="AllIcons.Actions.Edit"
        >
            <keyboard-shortcut keymap="$default" first-keystroke="shift F6"/>
        </action>
    </actions>

    <!-- close listeners -->
    <applicationListeners>

        <listener
                class="org.testin.editor.open.SaveOnProjectClose"
                topic="com.intellij.openapi.project.ProjectCloseListener"
        />

    </applicationListeners>

    <projectListeners>

        <listener
                class="org.testin.editor.open.OpenEditorsFollowTheIndex"
                topic="org.testin.indexer.IndexChanged"
        />

        <listener
                class="org.testin.git.history.RecordsFollowTheIndex"
                topic="org.testin.indexer.IndexChanged"
        />

    </projectListeners>

</idea-plugin>
